  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1235 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nAnother rinse and repeat forecast today as hot and humid\nconditions persist across the Mid-South. A subtropical ridge,\ncurrently centered over the Texas coastline, continues to broaden\nnorthwest as Tropical Depression 2 proceeds to churn near the\nFlorida Peninsula. These two systems have placed us, here in the\nMid-South, under very weak northwest flow aloft while at the\nsurface we remain under southwest flow with high pressure to our\neast. The airmass remains very moist with current PWATs in the\n1.8-2.2\" range and dewpoints in the mid to upper 70s. Afternoon\nhighs will span the low to upper 90s warranting a Heat Advisory\nthat will remain in effect through 9PM this evening for areas\nwest of the Mississippi River and portions of north Mississippi\nand West Tennessee. Embedded shortwave perturbations, persistent\nmoisture, and peak daytime heating, will once again support pop-\nup showers and storms this afternoon into early evening.\n\nOppressive heat and humidity will continue and increase in\ncoverage and intensity tomorrow as the aforementioned ridge\nbroadens and nearly centers over the central High Plains.\nMoisture will also persist keeping our dewpoints high and\ntemperatures will increase with highs in the mid to upper 90s\nareawide. Areas in the Delta region will see the highest\ntemperatures and dewpoints warranting the issuance of an Extreme\nHeat Warning in these areas while the rest of the Mid-South will\nbe under a Heat Advisory tomorrow and Tuesday. One thing to note:\nNBM is currently painting heat indices in excess of 110+ F for\nmany areas west of the Mississippi River, though it looks like\nNBM continues to have a high bias in these areas. Both the Heat\nAdvisory and Extreme Heat Warning have been extended through\nTuesday evening with little to no relief overnight. Tuesday will\nbe the warmest over the next 7 days as the ridge begins to\nbroaden east into the Mississippi Valley increasing our highs\ninto the upper 90s across most of the Mid-South. Some areas could\neven see highs in the lower 100s, especially west of the\nMississippi River and in the Delta regions. The Extreme Heat\nWarning will likely be expanded further north and potentially\nfurther east for Tuesday. Be sure to take frequent breaks from\nthe oppressive heat and check in on loved ones over the next two\ndays.\n\nLuckily, relief will be on its way beginning Wednesday as an\nupper-level low begins to dip south from Ontario into the\nnortheast CONUS pushing us further into a northwest flow regime\naloft and the subtropical ridge back west. At the surface, a cold\nfront will begin to push across the Mid-South decreasing\ntemperatures to more comfortable levels for July. As this front\nbegins to dip down, the Storm Prediction Center has drawn a\nMarginal Risk for severe weather across portions of West\nTennessee for mainly Tuesday evening into potentially overnight\nWednesday. We're a little too far out to pinpoint storm mode,\nLREF joint probs are hinting at around a 45-60% chance for\neffective shear >25 kts and SBCAPE > 1200 J/kg in this area. For\nright now, there exists a conditional chance for a few strong to\nsevere thunderstorms with primary concern of damaging winds.\nMoving into the latter half of the week, we're looking wet and\nunsettled as northwest flow aloft persists, though for right now,\nheat remains the primary concern.\n\nAEH",
